## Your lifts

Progress lists every exercise you've done at least twice, with its trend and a chart of your estimated
one-rep max. On the web (Pro) you can put several lifts on one chart with Compare.

## Progression

After each workout, LogIt suggests the next targets for each exercise. How it does that is up to you,
in Settings › Progression:

- **LogIt Adaptive** (the default) plans every set from your current strength: the best estimated
  one-rep max from your last few sessions. The weight is the heaviest that fits the rep range (5–8 for
  big lifts, 8–12 for single-muscle lifts, close to failure, all changeable), and the reps per set allow
  for how much yours usually drop from set to set. Light warm-up or back-off sets don't hold it back, and
  beating the planned reps raises the weight next time. Tap **Why?** on an exercise to see the numbers.
- **Linear Progression** is the simple option: one more rep each time, and 2.5 kg more once every set
  hits the top of the range.
- **Disable suggestions** turns planning off; you add sets yourself.

You can also install a progression plugin.

Any exercise can be set differently: open it and use **How it progresses** to give it its own rep
range, its own algorithm (or no suggestions), and, if it has a lift goal, choose what happens when you
fall behind pace: just show it, or push the first set a little heavier (LogIt Adaptive). A split day
that sets its own reps still wins on that day. These settings sync with Pro.
